<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etowah Indian Mounds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000–1550</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Etowah Mounds</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Etowah Indian Mounds, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartersville-georgia">Cartersville, Georgia</a>, are the remains of a Mississippian town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etowah-river">Etowah River</a>, occupied in three phases between about 1000 and 1550. Three main platform mounds, the largest 19 metres high, stand beside a plaza, and the town was at its peak about 1325–1375. Elite burials held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> tools, weapons and ornamental plates, and finds from Mound C helped define the Southeastern Ceremonial Complex. Paired stone statues of a seated man and a kneeling woman, found in the area, are thought to represent lineage ancestors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etowah-indian-mounds.jpg" alt="Etowah Indian Mounds" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kåre Thor Olsen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USA-Georgia-Etowah_Indian_Mounds-Mound_B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
